---
import copy from '../../data/home-copy.json';
import { FOOTER_COLUMNS, TRADEMARK_NOTICE } from '../../lib/footer';
import { CHROME_STORE_URL } from '../../lib/site';

// Prerendered marketing content for `/`.
//
// This is SEO surface, not UI: apps/worker/web/main.tsx clears #app on boot
// (`root.innerHTML = ''`) and renders <Landing/> over it, so nobody with JS
// ever sees this markup. That is exactly how it drifted — it used to live in
// apps/worker/index.html and went months without review. Keep the claims here
// in sync with apps/worker/web/Landing.tsx.
---

<p style="margin: 0 0 12px; color: #6e6e6e">Free forever. No sign-up. Open source.</p>
<h1>{`${copy.headlinePrefix} ${copy.headlineJoiner} ${copy.headlineChannel} ${copy.headlineSuffix}`}</h1>
<p>
  Send your client one link. They comment straight on the live page in their own browser, without signing up or
  installing anything.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>MarkLayer is a free web app and Chrome extension</strong> for annotating any live webpage, PDF or image.
  Paste any URL on
  <a href="https://marklayer.app">marklayer.app</a> to draw and comment in your browser. No install required. Or use
  the free Chrome extension for in-place annotation on sites behind a login or with strict embed policies. Share a
  single link. Viewers need no extension or account.
  <strong
    >You never create an account or pay for a plan. Annotations are cleaned up 90 days after their last activity, and
    any comment or view resets that clock.</strong
  >
</p>
<form action="/proxy" method="get" role="search" aria-label="Annotate a webpage by URL">
  <label for="ml-url-input">Paste any URL to annotate in your browser</label>
  <input
    id="ml-url-input"
    name="url"
    type="url"
    inputmode="url"
    autocomplete="url"
    placeholder="https://example.com"
    required
  />
  <button type="submit">Start Annotating</button>
  <p>No install. No account. No sign-up.</p>
</form>
<p>
  <a href={CHROME_STORE_URL}
    >Or add the free Chrome extension</a
  > for sites the web app cannot embed.
</p>

<h2>Free + Anonymous, by design</h2>
<p>
  Most annotation tools force you to create an account, hand over an
  email, and onboard before you can mark up a single page. MarkLayer
  skips all of that.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>100% free.</strong> No paid plan, no trial period, no
    per-seat pricing, no usage cap, no premium tier. See the full
    <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>100% anonymous.</strong> No sign-up, no email
    verification, no profile, no login. A random local display name is
    generated in your browser.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>No data collection.</strong> Annotations stay on your
    device until you explicitly share them.
    <a href="/privacy">Privacy policy</a>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Open source.</strong> Audit the code, contribute, or
    self-host on
    <a href="https://github.com/thevrus/MarkLayer">GitHub</a>.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>Features</h2>
<p>
  Everything below is free, with no account. The
  <a href="/features">full feature list</a> has the detail, including
  every keyboard shortcut.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Sixteen annotation tools.</strong> Pen, highlighter,
    arrow, line, rectangle, circle, text label, eraser, comment pin,
    text selection, area note, element inspector, multi-select,
    measure, ruler guides and move. Each has a single-key shortcut,
    matching Figma where Figma has an equivalent.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Comments with status, priority and an owner.</strong> Five
    statuses (open, in progress, resolved, approved, dismissed), four
    priorities, threaded replies, @mentions, and up to four image
    attachments per comment. Each one records the browser, OS and
    viewport it was written on.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Suggested edits as diffs.</strong> Select text on the page
    and write the exact replacement. It travels as a diff into the
    export and into an AI agent's view, so nobody retypes it.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Real-time collaboration.</strong> Live cursors so everyone
    sees changes as they happen. Unlimited collaborators per session.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Voice and video.</strong> Peer-to-peer WebRTC in the room,
    with microphone, camera and output device selection. Nobody
    installs anything to join.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Shareable links.</strong> Share a link so anyone can see
    your annotations instantly. No extension and no account needed to
    view. Sign in with a magic link, and you can make a link view
    only, set its expiry, and see everything you own in one dashboard.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Send feedback to Slack, Teams, Discord, Linear, GitHub or
    Jira.</strong> Chat destinations post each new batch on their own.
    File a single annotation into a tracker as an issue. It is one way
    out: nothing syncs back.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Export as Markdown or PNG.</strong> Copy or download every
    comment, reply and highlight as Markdown, with suggested edits as
    diff blocks. Or export the annotated page as an image.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Built for AI coding agents.</strong> The inspector
    captures an element's CSS selector, computed styles and the React
    or Vue component behind it, as a copy-ready prompt. The
    <code>marklayer-mcp</code> server gives an agent twelve tools to
    watch a room, acknowledge, resolve, dismiss and reply, with every
    status change visible to the human.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Works on any website.</strong> Production, staging,
    internal tools, localhost, third-party sites. Anything you can
    open in Chrome.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>PDFs and images.</strong> Open a PDF, a screenshot, a
    mockup or a photo by link, or upload one up to 25MB. PDF text
    stays selectable, so a highlight binds to the words. See
    <a href="/guides/how-to-annotate-a-pdf">how to annotate a PDF</a>
    and <a href="/guides/how-to-annotate-an-image">how to annotate an
    image</a>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Up to 50 pages on one link.</strong> Review a whole flow
    as a project, switched through tabs, exported as one document.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Marks that survive a reflow.</strong> Preview at desktop,
    tablet or mobile width. Comments bind to the element, by selector
    with a text fingerprint as a fallback, so they land on the right
    thing when the layout changes or a single-page app re-renders.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Browser extension.</strong> One click to start annotating.
    No background processes, no permissions creep.
  </li>
</ul>

